  "summary": "Art where the concept or idea behind the work is more important than traditional aesthetic qualities.",
  "description": "Often employs text, documentation, and non-traditional materials to convey ideas, rejecting traditional art forms. It emerged as a reaction against commercialization of art and emphasis on technical skill.",
  "culturalContext": "In 1961, philosopher and artist Henry Flynt coined the term \"concept art\" in an article bearing the same name which appeared in the proto-Fluxus publication An Anthology of Chance Operations. Flynt's concept art, he maintained, devolved from his notion of \"cognitive nihilism\", in which paradoxes in logic are shown to evacuate concepts of substance. Drawing on the syntax of logic and mathematics, concept art was meant jointly to supersede mathematics and the formalistic music then current in serious art music circles. Therefore, Flynt maintained, to merit the label concept art, a work had to be a critique of logic or mathematics in which a linguistic concept was the material, a quality which is absent from subsequent \"conceptual art\".",
